parts.body:
[[keogh-plan|Keogh plans]] (also called HR-10 plans) are retirement plans for **self-employed individuals and unincorporated businesses** such as sole proprietorships and partnerships. The name comes from the law that first let unincorporated businesses sponsor retirement plans. Because the law no longer distinguishes corporate from unincorporated sponsors, the term is now seldom used — these are simply qualified plans set up by the self-employed, and eligible employees must be covered too.

A [[sep-plan|Simplified Employee Pension (SEP)]] is often the simplest choice for the self-employed and small firms:

- any size business, even a self-employed person with no employees, can set one up;
- **only the employer contributes**, to a traditional IRA (SEP-IRA) for each eligible employee;
- the employer **doesn't have to contribute every year**, but when it does, it must contribute equally for all eligible employees;
- contributions are always 100% vested.
